198
00:11:32,818 --> 00:11:33,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course,

199
00:11:33,985 --> 00:11:36,306
[SPEAKER_01]: pulled me, said, well, we'll arrest him.

200
00:11:36,326 --> 00:11:38,027
[SPEAKER_01]: We're going to be using him in the World Series.

201
00:11:39,288 --> 00:11:42,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So Newsom went in, and of course, they finally beat us 2 to 1.

202
00:11:42,409 --> 00:11:43,770
[SPEAKER_01]: He allowed two runs.

203
00:11:43,790 --> 00:11:44,590
[SPEAKER_01]: We lost a game.

204
00:11:45,931 --> 00:11:47,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You had the pennant won, though.

205
00:11:47,712 --> 00:11:49,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you really bear down on those games?

206
00:11:49,793 --> 00:11:51,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, my goodness, yes.

207
00:11:51,054 --> 00:11:53,615
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to bear down and get your brains beat out.

208
00:11:53,895 --> 00:11:57,797
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a matter of self-preservation, surely.

209
00:11:57,837 --> 00:12:00,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Boy, I wasn't happy after that Eagle game.

210
00:12:00,319 --> 00:12:02,880
[SPEAKER_01]: All us Cleveland fans, we were just

211
00:12:03,323 --> 00:12:05,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's just the way it was.

212
00:12:05,104 --> 00:12:08,367
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, Cleveland, I think, was all tightened up and happy.

213
00:12:08,447 --> 00:12:10,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Couldn't hit with the other hand.

214
00:12:10,769 --> 00:12:13,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a year of the crybabies and everything.

215
00:12:13,551 --> 00:12:16,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Oscar Viet and the ball players, I guess, fell out.

216
00:12:16,253 --> 00:12:20,757
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I said, were you with them when they, in Cleveland, they dumped the fruit on Bertie Tebbets?

217
00:12:20,797 --> 00:12:20,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

218
00:12:21,357 --> 00:12:23,579
[SPEAKER_00]: I was at that game, I think, or right in there.

219
00:12:23,599 --> 00:12:28,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I was sitting right alongside of Tebbets when he got hit with that basket of fruit from the upper deck.

220
00:12:29,610 --> 00:12:41,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And it knocked him out, really, because that basket, he was sitting on a bench, you know where the bullpen was in the stadium, down the left field line, and he was sitting on that low bench and that basket of fruit hit him right across the head and the shoulders.

221
00:12:42,079 --> 00:12:43,720
[SPEAKER_01]: He just fell flat on his face.

222
00:12:44,661 --> 00:12:46,042
[SPEAKER_01]: You were at least in the bullpen then?

223
00:12:46,142 --> 00:12:47,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

224
00:12:47,302 --> 00:12:49,304
[SPEAKER_01]: That basket of fruit came from the second deck.

225
00:12:51,125 --> 00:12:52,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, look what they throw now.

226
00:12:52,706 --> 00:12:54,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Basket of fruit is quaint.

227
00:12:54,467 --> 00:12:56,089
[SPEAKER_00]: I was at that game in Cleveland a couple weeks ago.

228
00:12:57,329 --> 00:12:59,811
[SPEAKER_00]: riot and all the cherry bomb and so on.

229
00:13:00,611 --> 00:13:02,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Things have changed, huh?

230
00:13:02,572 --> 00:13:03,433
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd say they have.

231
00:13:04,093 --> 00:13:06,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, your best year was, which would you say here?

232
00:13:07,155 --> 00:13:09,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, 41, 9-11.

233
00:13:10,157 --> 00:13:14,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I had 7-7 the first year, 9-11 the second year.

234
00:13:14,279 --> 00:13:18,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Then after that, you remember, I held out in 41, 42.

235
00:13:18,782 --> 00:13:20,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, tell me about that.

236
00:13:21,977 --> 00:13:23,738
[SPEAKER_01]: I held out for a little race and didn't get it.

237
00:13:24,478 --> 00:13:26,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you negotiate with Jack Zeller?

238
00:13:26,879 --> 00:13:29,320
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I was negotiating with Jack Zeller.

239
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:31,581
[SPEAKER_00]: When did you report?

240
00:13:31,761 --> 00:13:31,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Late?

241
00:13:32,061 --> 00:13:36,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yes, I reported April 1st, but in the meantime I was conditioning here.

242
00:13:37,503 --> 00:13:41,484
[SPEAKER_01]: When I got that spring training about April 1st, I was in good shape to pitch.

243
00:13:41,644 --> 00:13:42,605
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't really miss much?

244
00:13:42,745 --> 00:13:47,907
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't miss much, but I was always a bug on conditioning.

245
00:13:47,927 --> 00:13:49,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You look in great shape now.

246
00:13:49,127 --> 00:13:50,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go out there and pitch today.

247
00:13:51,620 --> 00:13:53,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

248
00:13:53,862 --> 00:13:55,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody fooled me.

249
00:13:55,683 --> 00:13:58,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Probably the bowling helps keep you in shape.

250
00:13:58,405 --> 00:14:01,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I bowl, but I watch myself.

251
00:14:01,767 --> 00:14:05,490
[SPEAKER_00]: What was your playing weight compared to now?

252
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:10,374
[SPEAKER_01]: My playing weight when I had to hang up the spikes was about 185.

253
00:14:10,434 --> 00:14:12,255
[SPEAKER_01]: I weigh 200 right now.

254
00:14:13,076 --> 00:14:20,421
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people probably wouldn't see the difference because in a uniform you look a little bigger.

255
00:14:20,710 --> 00:14:24,731
[SPEAKER_01]: The uniforms we wore in those days were loose.

256
00:14:26,552 --> 00:14:28,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Today they use them skin tight.

257
00:14:28,512 --> 00:14:29,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I just don't know how they can do it.

258
00:14:30,493 --> 00:14:32,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think that might constrict their movement some?

259
00:14:32,993 --> 00:14:38,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to me it felt like it bound me if I had it tight on.

260
00:14:40,215 --> 00:14:41,095
[SPEAKER_01]: We liked them loose.

261
00:14:42,816 --> 00:14:43,596
[SPEAKER_00]: They were cooler.

262
00:14:44,456 --> 00:14:46,877
[SPEAKER_00]: What did Zeller say when you

263
00:14:47,895 --> 00:14:51,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let me just say, I hadn't proved myself.

264
00:14:51,138 --> 00:14:54,701
[SPEAKER_01]: I had to spend another year, have another winning season.

265
00:14:54,721 --> 00:14:58,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm like, I was just bad salesman, that's all.

266
00:14:58,865 --> 00:15:02,589
[SPEAKER_00]: How was Del Baker, co-manager then?

267
00:15:02,629 --> 00:15:04,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Del Baker was a good manager.

268
00:15:04,271 --> 00:15:06,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Del was a good manager.

269
00:15:06,433 --> 00:15:07,333
[SPEAKER_01]: He was good to me.

270
00:15:08,935 --> 00:15:11,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And I thought Del was a good manager.

271
00:15:12,938 --> 00:15:18,139
[SPEAKER_01]: because he'd been a coach with Detroit for many years before he was a manager before I even arrived.

272
00:15:18,919 --> 00:15:22,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, I had no squabbles with management.

273
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:24,381
[SPEAKER_01]: I never had a problem with a manager.

274
00:15:25,181 --> 00:15:29,082
[SPEAKER_01]: The only problem I had was with Zeller that one year, and that was it.

275
00:15:29,922 --> 00:15:32,843
[SPEAKER_00]: How was the spirit on the Detroit team, say, in 40?

276
00:15:33,203 --> 00:15:34,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it was great.

277
00:15:34,523 --> 00:15:35,763
[SPEAKER_01]: It was great.

278
00:15:37,704 --> 00:15:39,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You could see the air about the boys.

279
00:15:39,504 --> 00:15:41,825
[SPEAKER_01]: They had confidence that they were going to win a pennant.

280
00:15:42,958 --> 00:15:55,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you remember back in that year, we went into the last month of the season, Labor Day, and we were three and a half games out of first place.

281
00:15:56,621 --> 00:16:03,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think we won about 17 out of 20 that year, that month, and went into first place.

282
00:16:04,474 --> 00:16:07,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was the big winning pitcher, Bridges?

283
00:16:09,197 --> 00:16:10,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Buck Newsom.

284
00:16:10,898 --> 00:16:13,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Buck, I think, won 21 of 22 games.

285
00:16:13,460 --> 00:16:15,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And Bridges won about 15.

286
00:16:15,361 --> 00:16:18,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Schoolboy Rowe won about 16 that year.

287
00:16:18,503 --> 00:16:19,624
[SPEAKER_01]: That could come back.

288
00:16:20,064 --> 00:16:22,566
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, we had Al Neuhauser.

289
00:16:22,586 --> 00:16:23,987
[SPEAKER_01]: He won 9 and 9.

290
00:16:24,907 --> 00:16:26,248
[SPEAKER_01]: I won 7 and 7.

291
00:16:27,849 --> 00:16:29,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Dizzy Trout had a bad year.

292
00:16:30,131 --> 00:16:31,812
[SPEAKER_01]: He only won three games that year.

293
00:16:33,470 --> 00:16:35,111
[SPEAKER_01]: But his problem was his weight.

294
00:16:36,391 --> 00:16:39,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It was mid-season before he could even get in shape.

295
00:16:39,253 --> 00:16:41,894
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd taken a job selling beer the winter before, and...

296
00:16:43,514 --> 00:16:44,975
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he thought he had the drink of it.

297
00:16:44,995 --> 00:16:47,116
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess after retirement, he really ballooned, too, didn't he?

298
00:16:47,156 --> 00:16:50,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, evidently so, but, uh... Three hundred pounds, yeah.

299
00:16:51,018 --> 00:16:51,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That trout?

300
00:16:51,958 --> 00:16:52,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he get off that beer?

301
00:16:52,858 --> 00:16:55,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Paul Frizz, a friend of mine in Terre Haute, where the trout was from.

302
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:57,961
[SPEAKER_00]: He knew him, and I think I told you something.

303
00:16:58,821 --> 00:17:03,163
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't believe it, because I hadn't seen Diz for many years, and only recently.

304
00:17:03,992 --> 00:17:08,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think he died about a year or two ago, and he was living in Chicago.

305
00:17:10,739 --> 00:17:12,021
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he was a sportscaster.

306
00:17:12,041 --> 00:17:15,785
[SPEAKER_00]: That may be high, but he put on some weight.

307
00:17:15,885 --> 00:17:21,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Bobo Newsom, was he the great jollifier that he was painted?

308
00:17:23,621 --> 00:17:24,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it this way.

309
00:17:25,142 --> 00:17:27,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Bobo liked his good times.

310
00:17:28,624 --> 00:17:32,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Off the field, on the field.

311
00:17:32,266 --> 00:17:33,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Was he a good influence?

312
00:17:35,688 --> 00:17:40,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, anybody that wins ball games on that ball field will be a good influence.

313
00:17:41,552 --> 00:17:44,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, other than that, I never saw him off the field.

314
00:17:44,634 --> 00:17:48,176
[SPEAKER_01]: I never associated with him.

315
00:17:52,773 --> 00:17:57,255
[SPEAKER_01]: All I know is what I heard, and that's immaterial.

316
00:17:57,976 --> 00:18:07,280
[SPEAKER_01]: But the way a man moves around like that, there must have been some grounds for his opinion, so I don't know.

317
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:11,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it Newsom's father who died just before he pitched that claim?

318
00:18:12,868 --> 00:18:14,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, his father died.

319
00:18:14,249 --> 00:18:17,131
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw his father the night before in the elevator.

320
00:18:17,151 --> 00:18:22,114
[SPEAKER_01]: We went up the elevator together at the old Netherland Plaza, which is now, what is it, Netherland Hilton.

321
00:18:23,035 --> 00:18:28,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And he died that night in Duck Pits, I think, the next day.

322
00:18:28,878 --> 00:18:29,719
[SPEAKER_00]: And he won, didn't he?

323
00:18:29,799 --> 00:18:30,659
[SPEAKER_00]: And he won.

324
00:18:30,679 --> 00:18:33,201
[SPEAKER_00]: That must have been an emotional moment.

325
00:18:33,341 --> 00:18:35,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that was something.

326
00:18:37,402 --> 00:18:39,122
[SPEAKER_01]: But those things happen, I guess.

327
00:18:39,163 --> 00:18:42,003
[SPEAKER_00]: I wonder how the Cincinnati players played that game.

328
00:18:42,063 --> 00:18:44,264
[SPEAKER_00]: They were kind of hard to beat, too, I expect.

329
00:18:44,724 --> 00:18:46,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably, but...

330
00:18:46,745 --> 00:18:49,285
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no psychology to pitch Newsom because of that, anyway.

331
00:18:49,885 --> 00:18:52,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Well...

332
00:18:53,607 --> 00:18:57,608
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't one to start, and he was the right one, so we went along with him.

333
00:18:58,368 --> 00:18:59,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you know Tommy Bridges very well?

334
00:19:00,608 --> 00:19:04,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Tommy was... Tommy was a good fellow.

335
00:19:04,950 --> 00:19:06,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Tommy was a good pitcher.

336
00:19:10,877 --> 00:19:11,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Very quiet.

337
00:19:11,798 --> 00:19:13,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Tommy didn't associate too much.

338
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:18,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was very capable.

339
00:19:19,941 --> 00:19:22,182
[SPEAKER_01]: He worked his one game a week because of his age.

340
00:19:22,222 --> 00:19:23,323
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess Tommy was 35.

341
00:19:23,363 --> 00:19:26,384
[SPEAKER_01]: He pitched one a week.

342
00:19:27,165 --> 00:19:28,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But he always gave a good performance.

343
00:19:29,766 --> 00:19:31,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Tommy was a real competitor.

344
00:19:33,350 --> 00:19:35,050
[SPEAKER_00]: What was your outfield in 40?

345
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:38,751
[SPEAKER_01]: In 40, we had Barney McCoskey in center field.

346
00:19:38,771 --> 00:19:40,671
[SPEAKER_01]: We had Greenberg playing left field.

347
00:19:40,711 --> 00:19:41,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Rudy York was on first.

348
00:19:41,932 --> 00:19:46,353
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the year they moved Greenberg to left field, to make room for York.

349
00:19:47,313 --> 00:19:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You remember, about a year or two before that, they tried York at catching.

350
00:19:51,814 --> 00:19:54,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And he played third too, a little bit?

351
00:19:54,734 --> 00:19:55,034
[SPEAKER_00]: York?

352
00:19:55,174 --> 00:19:56,455
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't think so.

353
00:19:57,095 --> 00:19:58,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Not while I was there, anyway.

354
00:20:00,853 --> 00:20:08,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And I guess he just could move around back there, satisfy the management, so then put him back on first and move Greenberg to left.

355
00:20:08,818 --> 00:20:14,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep those big sticks in the lineup.

356
00:20:14,641 --> 00:20:17,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And in right field, we had Pete Fox and Bruce Campbell.

357
00:20:17,863 --> 00:20:19,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Bruce Campbell was left-handed hitter.

358
00:20:19,424 --> 00:20:21,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Pete Fox was a right-handed hitter.

359
00:20:21,846 --> 00:20:25,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Then on the bench, we had Earl Averill as a pinch hitter and utility outfielder.

360
00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:30,262
[SPEAKER_01]: And Tabitz was the catcher, Bertie Tabitz.

361
00:20:30,543 --> 00:20:35,406
[SPEAKER_01]: On third base, we had Pinky Higgins, and that's the year Bartell played short for us.

362
00:20:35,847 --> 00:20:37,968
[SPEAKER_01]: They got him from the Giants the year before.

363
00:20:38,088 --> 00:20:39,249
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen an owner stick there.

364
00:20:39,549 --> 00:20:42,852
[SPEAKER_01]: No, Marvin was with Boston, I think, in 39.

365
00:20:44,313 --> 00:20:45,934
[SPEAKER_01]: They got Higgins in 39.

366
00:20:46,915 --> 00:20:48,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Garinger was playing second.

367
00:20:48,436 --> 00:20:49,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was still there, wasn't he?

368
00:20:49,757 --> 00:20:51,598
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a mechanical man.

369
00:20:51,718 --> 00:20:53,880
[SPEAKER_01]: And Billy Sullivan was the utility player.

370
00:20:53,900 --> 00:20:56,202
[SPEAKER_00]: He caught and filled in the outfield, too.

371
00:20:56,530 --> 00:20:57,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty good hitter.

372
00:20:58,231 --> 00:21:00,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Did these older players give you encouragement in your first year?

373
00:21:01,113 --> 00:21:02,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

374
00:21:02,113 --> 00:21:02,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely.

375
00:21:03,474 --> 00:21:05,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they were always willing to help.

376
00:21:05,395 --> 00:21:07,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's one thing.

377
00:21:08,417 --> 00:21:12,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're on the same team when you're out for one purpose, to win.

378
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:14,981
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only way you can win is to play together.

379
00:21:16,422 --> 00:21:22,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they could see where they can help you, they were always willing to offer encouragement and advice.

380
00:21:26,827 --> 00:21:27,588
[SPEAKER_00]: What about 45?

381
00:21:27,648 --> 00:21:28,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Were you in service that year?

382
00:21:29,389 --> 00:21:30,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I went in service in spring of 45.

383
00:21:30,991 --> 00:21:35,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think that had any effect on your career?

384
00:21:36,376 --> 00:21:37,577
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't think so.

385
00:21:37,597 --> 00:21:38,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you pitch in service?

386
00:21:39,399 --> 00:21:41,601
[SPEAKER_01]: I pitched with Great Lakes with Bob Fellow.

387
00:21:42,161 --> 00:21:46,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, we had Pinky Higgins in service at the time, too, and Walkie Cooper.

388
00:21:47,127 --> 00:21:49,049
[SPEAKER_00]: You must have won some titles up there.

389
00:21:49,309 --> 00:21:52,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think we lost one game this summer.

390
00:21:52,331 --> 00:21:55,072
[SPEAKER_00]: That's quite a team.

391
00:21:55,112 --> 00:21:59,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, of course, we played exhibition games on the station up there.

392
00:21:59,175 --> 00:22:02,177
[SPEAKER_01]: We played some major league ball clubs.

393
00:22:03,458 --> 00:22:07,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And Fella was doing all the pitching on the main station.

394
00:22:07,320 --> 00:22:11,763
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, when we got off the station and played away from it, why then we wouldn't be doing the pitching.

395
00:22:12,984 --> 00:22:14,985
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was playing in field and outfield.

396
00:22:16,425 --> 00:22:22,487
[SPEAKER_01]: I found a lakes team because Fowler did all the pitching at home and we were short of ballplayers.

397
00:22:22,507 --> 00:22:25,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'd play first base, I'd play shortstop.

398
00:22:25,088 --> 00:22:26,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably enjoyed a chance to play first base.

399
00:22:26,548 --> 00:22:27,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Played the outfield.

400
00:22:27,409 --> 00:22:30,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Did it every day, huh?

401
00:22:30,390 --> 00:22:34,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Just thinking back the other day, Dizzy Dean died.

402
00:22:34,971 --> 00:22:43,354
[SPEAKER_01]: We played an exhibition game in Moline, Illinois.

403
00:22:45,395 --> 00:22:47,076
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was pitching against us.

404
00:22:48,057 --> 00:22:49,158
[SPEAKER_01]: A fellow was pitching.

405
00:22:49,218 --> 00:22:51,359
[SPEAKER_01]: A fellow was managing the ball club.

406
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:54,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was playing the outfield.

407
00:22:55,322 --> 00:23:01,306
[SPEAKER_01]: We had John McKetty pitching, who used to be with the Giants at one time.

408
00:23:03,228 --> 00:23:05,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And I had a home run off Dizzy Dean.

409
00:23:08,340 --> 00:23:09,680
[SPEAKER_01]: The fella couldn't take that.

410
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:13,282
[SPEAKER_01]: He thought he was a good hitter, so he takes me out and puts himself in.

411
00:23:13,742 --> 00:23:21,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And he and Dizzy Dean had a real gab fest.

412
00:23:21,904 --> 00:23:24,865
[SPEAKER_01]: I could swing the stick pretty good for a pitcher if I wanted to.

413
00:23:26,486 --> 00:23:27,466
[SPEAKER_01]: I soon found out.

414
00:23:27,886 --> 00:23:31,828
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't up there very long when they told me, you're not getting paid for hitting, you're getting paid for pitching.

415
00:23:34,128 --> 00:23:36,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, did you, were you in service part of 46 too?

416
00:23:37,531 --> 00:23:39,253
[SPEAKER_01]: I got out in the spring of 46.

417
00:23:39,473 --> 00:23:42,376
[SPEAKER_01]: I was in exactly one year.

418
00:23:42,416 --> 00:23:49,563
[SPEAKER_01]: When I came out I had an acute, acute prostatitis and that delayed me reporting for about two months.

419
00:23:50,524 --> 00:23:52,526
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't join the club till about June 1st.

420
00:23:54,668 --> 00:23:56,290
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't pitch much that year?

421
00:23:56,550 --> 00:23:57,311
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I didn't.

422
00:23:57,331 --> 00:23:58,212
[SPEAKER_01]: I just relieved.

423
00:23:59,795 --> 00:24:03,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Just relieving probably pinch rent for somebody when they needed me.

424
00:24:03,637 --> 00:24:05,158
[SPEAKER_01]: O'Neal was managing them.

425
00:24:05,338 --> 00:24:06,419
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd you get along with Steve?

426
00:24:06,519 --> 00:24:08,100
[SPEAKER_01]: I got along all right with him.

427
00:24:08,540 --> 00:24:10,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Steve was a jolly Irishman.

428
00:24:10,041 --> 00:24:10,981
[SPEAKER_00]: He was okay.

429
00:24:12,722 --> 00:24:15,984
[SPEAKER_00]: In 47 you had a 2-0 there.

430
00:24:16,364 --> 00:24:19,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I was doing mostly relief work.

431
00:24:22,101 --> 00:24:25,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Didn't get too much work because they had some other youngsters come along.

432
00:24:25,443 --> 00:24:27,344
[SPEAKER_00]: By that time, I'm 35 years old.

433
00:24:27,925 --> 00:24:32,427
[SPEAKER_01]: See, I was 32 then.

434
00:24:32,968 --> 00:24:37,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Newhauser's great years in the war, you know, he won 29.

435
00:24:37,730 --> 00:24:41,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Was he really that great a pitcher, or was it the war years?

436
00:24:41,753 --> 00:24:42,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, no.

437
00:24:42,793 --> 00:24:48,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Newhauser was probably the best left-hander that I had ever seen when he was in his heyday.

438
00:24:49,675 --> 00:24:50,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Hal pitched great ball.

439
00:24:50,895 --> 00:24:53,656
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a good curve, a good change, and a good fastball.

440
00:24:53,756 --> 00:24:54,936
[SPEAKER_01]: And he could pitch.

441
00:24:54,996 --> 00:24:55,956
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't make any difference.

442
00:24:55,996 --> 00:24:57,216
[SPEAKER_01]: War years won them.

443
00:24:57,656 --> 00:24:58,737
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't make any difference.

444
00:24:58,777 --> 00:25:00,777
[SPEAKER_01]: It just happened that he did have his great years.

445
00:25:00,837 --> 00:25:03,397
[SPEAKER_01]: But I would say that Newhouse could win any time.

446
00:25:04,297 --> 00:25:06,958
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't last too much after the war.

447
00:25:07,278 --> 00:25:09,658
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what makes you won one little game.

448
00:25:09,878 --> 00:25:19,320
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that his trouble probably could be pinpointed to the fact that he was pitching so much during the war years because of the lack of good pitches in their

449
00:25:20,748 --> 00:25:21,929
[SPEAKER_01]: attempt to win the pennants.

450
00:25:22,229 --> 00:25:26,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in 1944, for instance, he and Trout were the workhorses of the staff.

451
00:25:26,693 --> 00:25:27,614
[SPEAKER_01]: One won, what, 28?

452
00:25:27,734 --> 00:25:32,178
[SPEAKER_01]: The other one won 29 or something like that.

453
00:25:32,198 --> 00:25:32,678
[SPEAKER_00]: 27, 29.

454
00:25:32,738 --> 00:25:34,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they're way up there.

455
00:25:35,601 --> 00:25:38,543
[SPEAKER_01]: And by the time the end of the season rolled around, they were fagged out.

456
00:25:38,623 --> 00:25:40,985
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, they were worn out from the work so much.

457
00:25:41,005 --> 00:25:43,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So the man just threw himself out in like four years.

458
00:25:43,247 --> 00:25:44,188
[SPEAKER_01]: That's probably it.

459
00:25:44,949 --> 00:25:47,771
[SPEAKER_01]: And that hard work takes its toll.

460
00:25:47,831 --> 00:25:48,652
[SPEAKER_01]: I found that out.

461
00:25:50,870 --> 00:25:59,120
[SPEAKER_01]: My name probably isn't in the records as much as it could have been, but it doesn't mean I wasn't out there throwing.

462
00:26:00,362 --> 00:26:02,684
[SPEAKER_01]: I can remember one day, one stretch,

463
00:26:03,759 --> 00:26:10,703
[SPEAKER_01]: where I got up 18 straight days to pitch, get ready to pitch and go in, and not get in the ballgame.

464
00:26:11,584 --> 00:26:13,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And that takes something out of you.

465
00:26:13,765 --> 00:26:15,186
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, I've often wondered about that.

466
00:26:15,246 --> 00:26:17,587
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, just watching the games on television, you see the...

467
00:26:17,967 --> 00:26:20,769
[SPEAKER_02]: I think those poor men are going to be worn out before they ever get in.

468
00:26:20,829 --> 00:26:27,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you see, those things happen, and that's something... Well, now you can see it, but in those days before television, you couldn't see it.

469
00:26:28,793 --> 00:26:31,554
[SPEAKER_01]: So it didn't impress you.

470
00:26:31,594 --> 00:26:33,154
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you see what's going on.

471
00:26:33,194 --> 00:26:38,196
[SPEAKER_01]: You wonder, well, there's a fellow warmed up last night, ready to get in the ballgame, and today he's in the ballgame.

472
00:26:38,216 --> 00:26:41,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe he recovered enough.

473
00:26:41,957 --> 00:26:47,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Chances are if that continues, about the second or third time he does that, he's going to lose his edge.

474
00:26:48,979 --> 00:26:54,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And he might make that bad pitch when he wants to make a good pitch, and it ends up out of the park and the game's over.

475
00:26:54,968 --> 00:26:56,429
[SPEAKER_00]: What was your best pitch?

476
00:26:56,669 --> 00:26:59,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I was strictly a sinker ball pitcher.

477
00:27:00,251 --> 00:27:02,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But I threw a sinker ball overhanded.

478
00:27:04,014 --> 00:27:05,435
[SPEAKER_00]: That curves and drops?

479
00:27:05,575 --> 00:27:06,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

480
00:27:06,235 --> 00:27:08,737
[SPEAKER_01]: A ball, it would come and go down, see?

481
00:27:09,398 --> 00:27:10,638
[SPEAKER_01]: It would go straight down.

482
00:27:11,739 --> 00:27:15,181
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'd come out of it this way.

483
00:27:15,241 --> 00:27:18,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'd kick it with the thumb, see?

484
00:27:18,424 --> 00:27:20,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Give it the spin to bring it straight.

485
00:27:20,525 --> 00:27:23,407
[SPEAKER_01]: And that ball would just seem like it just dropped straight down.

486
00:27:24,985 --> 00:27:27,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, most think a ball will throw and go like that.

487
00:27:28,228 --> 00:27:29,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you develop it pretty much yourself?

488
00:27:29,909 --> 00:27:30,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, yeah.

489
00:27:32,531 --> 00:27:33,732
[SPEAKER_00]: How about a fastball?

490
00:27:33,752 --> 00:27:37,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, before I signed up to play pro ball, I pitched in high school.

491
00:27:37,875 --> 00:27:39,997
[SPEAKER_01]: I pitched semi-pro.

492
00:27:41,038 --> 00:27:42,279
[SPEAKER_01]: I played all over just to play.

493
00:27:42,299 --> 00:27:43,980
[SPEAKER_01]: There was some background for making the pitch.

494
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:44,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, surely.

495
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:46,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

496
00:27:47,983 --> 00:27:53,727
[SPEAKER_01]: If I hadn't had the background, I probably wouldn't have developed fast enough to get to the big leagues in two years.

497
00:27:55,090 --> 00:27:58,913
[SPEAKER_00]: So, who was, did you have a toughest hitter to pitch against?

498
00:27:59,193 --> 00:28:02,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh my gosh, there were a load of them.

499
00:28:03,016 --> 00:28:06,658
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest one was that left-hander from Boston, Ted Williams.

500
00:28:07,219 --> 00:28:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: He was great.

501
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:08,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Terrific.

502
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:09,681
[SPEAKER_01]: How about DiMaggio?

503
00:28:10,261 --> 00:28:12,162
[SPEAKER_01]: DiMaggio was the toughest.

504
00:28:12,182 --> 00:28:13,563
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a tough right-handed hitter.

505
00:28:14,164 --> 00:28:19,668
[SPEAKER_01]: But there were so many tough right-handed hitters, but Williams as a left-hander will stand out.

506
00:28:20,740 --> 00:28:24,483
[SPEAKER_01]: in my mind, above all the left-handers and even right-handers for that matter.

507
00:28:24,543 --> 00:28:25,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Overall, he was the best.

508
00:28:26,745 --> 00:28:34,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you go on down the line in those days, you had hitters like Fox, Paul hitters, DiMaggio.

509
00:28:35,051 --> 00:28:37,593
[SPEAKER_01]: You had Joe Cronin playing short for Boston.

510
00:28:37,653 --> 00:28:43,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in his twilight years, he was still a good hitter.

511
00:28:43,297 --> 00:28:45,799
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a master at hitting, that Cronin was.

512
00:28:45,859 --> 00:28:48,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's the one that taught Williams

513
00:28:49,691 --> 00:28:58,358
[SPEAKER_01]: He and Harry Heilman, who was the broadcaster for Detroit, they've spent a lot of time with him, trying to impress upon him what he had to do.

514
00:28:59,359 --> 00:29:00,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Hitting is an art.

515
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:08,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You can develop a hitter if he wants to, has the reflexes.

516
00:29:08,086 --> 00:29:08,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like pitching.

517
00:29:09,087 --> 00:29:11,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it easier to pitch to right-handers and left-handers?

518
00:29:15,002 --> 00:29:20,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of them, you could say, are easier to pitch than others, but the plate's the same.

519
00:29:20,525 --> 00:29:21,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't move.

520
00:29:22,026 --> 00:29:25,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to get the ball over the plate to get somebody out.

521
00:29:25,888 --> 00:29:28,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So it really doesn't make any difference.

522
00:29:28,170 --> 00:29:29,070
[SPEAKER_00]: How's your control?

523
00:29:30,191 --> 00:29:33,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I expect about first year it was pretty rough.

524
00:29:34,173 --> 00:29:36,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You had more strikeouts than walks almost all the time.

525
00:29:37,788 --> 00:29:41,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So I guess you have to have 68, 57, 59, 55.

526
00:29:41,490 --> 00:29:46,532
[SPEAKER_00]: This is one year here, you're 28, 29, 30, 40, 45, 36.

527
00:29:46,552 --> 00:29:49,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're more strikeouts than most of the time.

528
00:29:49,853 --> 00:30:04,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, my control, you look at the records like that, and I'll say that it doesn't prove too much, because when you walk in from the bullpen and you've got men on base, you just can't throw the ball down the middle.

529
00:30:05,905 --> 00:30:09,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're not warmed up properly, or if you're not sharp that day, you're going to walk.

530
00:30:10,728 --> 00:30:13,690
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you're trying to do is keep that man from hitting that ball out of the park.

531
00:30:13,710 --> 00:30:18,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's swinging for the fence.

532
00:30:18,693 --> 00:30:21,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't try to give him anything too fat to hit at.

533
00:30:21,335 --> 00:30:23,096
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a lot of pressure on those relief pitches.

534
00:30:23,116 --> 00:30:23,576
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

535
00:30:23,596 --> 00:30:27,419
[SPEAKER_01]: You walk in there, and you've got one job to do, and that's get him out and make him hit your pitch.

536
00:30:27,499 --> 00:30:29,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you can't get it off the plate while you're in trouble.

537
00:30:30,961 --> 00:30:31,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So.

538
00:30:34,043 --> 00:30:34,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

539
00:30:36,223 --> 00:30:38,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what happened after 47?

540
00:30:38,083 --> 00:30:39,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you play more?

541
00:30:39,324 --> 00:30:54,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, at the end of season 47, I guess Billy Evans, who was general manager toward Barclay at the time, had made a deal with Seattle, whereas they got some ball player, I don't know who it was, and they had to send them somebody.

542
00:30:55,406 --> 00:31:02,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they had all these young kids coming up, and they had to get rid of somebody, and I just happened to be one of them.

543
00:31:02,107 --> 00:31:05,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So they sold me out to Seattle in part of a deal.

544
00:31:06,894 --> 00:31:08,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And I went out there and pitched that summer.

545
00:31:09,676 --> 00:31:10,957
[SPEAKER_01]: How things go there?

546
00:31:11,757 --> 00:31:15,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, a minor league is a minor league.

547
00:31:16,741 --> 00:31:20,183
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought the Pacific Coast League was a real fast league.

548
00:31:20,203 --> 00:31:22,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I got out there, they had some good ballplayers.

549
00:31:22,725 --> 00:31:25,166
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not taking anything away from them.

550
00:31:25,186 --> 00:31:28,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But my goodness, we had ballplayers who couldn't stop a balloon.

551
00:31:30,050 --> 00:31:32,051
[SPEAKER_01]: I swear, it was amazing.

552
00:31:32,091 --> 00:31:36,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you're used to having infielders and outfielders behind you,

553
00:31:36,801 --> 00:31:44,383
[SPEAKER_01]: can go get a ball or a ground ball and walk out there and they drop them right in their hands.

554
00:31:44,583 --> 00:31:47,383
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I won 13 games and lost 15.

555
00:31:49,284 --> 00:31:57,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And I bet I lost half of those losses in the ninth inning.

556
00:32:00,247 --> 00:32:06,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there you lose two in a week up there because you pitch on Sunday and you'll pitch maybe Thursday or Friday again or Wednesday.

557
00:32:07,683 --> 00:32:09,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that where you had the week-long series?

558
00:32:09,504 --> 00:32:10,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

559
00:32:10,324 --> 00:32:12,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You went in on Tuesday and you played through Sunday.

560
00:32:12,745 --> 00:32:17,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you went out of that town, Monday was off, and you went into the next town and played six, seven, eight.

561
00:32:17,906 --> 00:32:19,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you like that kind of arrangement?

562
00:32:19,567 --> 00:32:21,127
[SPEAKER_00]: A little less moving around?

563
00:32:21,327 --> 00:32:24,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it doesn't make any difference.

564
00:32:25,048 --> 00:32:28,329
[SPEAKER_01]: In those days, we played all-night baseball there except on the weekend.

565
00:32:30,178 --> 00:32:31,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't make any difference.

566
00:32:31,699 --> 00:32:33,041
[SPEAKER_00]: You had more time for yourself.

567
00:32:33,061 --> 00:32:34,182
[SPEAKER_00]: You had more time to recoup.

568
00:32:34,202 --> 00:32:36,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Did Mrs. Garcia go with you that year?

569
00:32:36,444 --> 00:32:37,786
[SPEAKER_01]: She brought the boy out.

570
00:32:37,806 --> 00:32:42,431
[SPEAKER_01]: My boy was born in 40 and they came out to Seattle that summer.

571
00:32:42,451 --> 00:32:47,296
[SPEAKER_01]: The first time they'd come out wherever I'd been and spent the summer when school was out.

572
00:32:48,732 --> 00:32:51,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So they, while I was on the road, they went sightseeing.

573
00:32:51,814 --> 00:32:54,656
[SPEAKER_02]: They got an education.

574
00:32:56,437 --> 00:33:01,520
[SPEAKER_01]: They had a real nice time, which I was happy because they couldn't do it before.

575
00:33:03,861 --> 00:33:05,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was your manager in Seattle?

576
00:33:06,043 --> 00:33:09,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Joe Joe White, the old outrigger.

577
00:33:09,625 --> 00:33:10,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Was he a teammate with you before?

578
00:33:11,006 --> 00:33:12,647
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he left before.

579
00:33:15,144 --> 00:33:17,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I forget where Jojo went from Detroit.

580
00:33:17,466 --> 00:33:18,626
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he went to St. Louis.

581
00:33:18,706 --> 00:33:19,467
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure.

582
00:33:19,767 --> 00:33:23,289
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a couple of real good minor leaguers after his major league career.

583
00:33:23,309 --> 00:33:27,952
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he won some most valuable player award in the American Association maybe.

584
00:33:27,972 --> 00:33:32,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, did you really enjoy playing ball?

585
00:33:33,776 --> 00:33:38,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I enjoyed it so much that I stayed in it as long as I was physically able.

586
00:33:39,660 --> 00:33:41,121
[SPEAKER_00]: How long did you stay after?

587
00:33:41,141 --> 00:33:42,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I...

588
00:33:43,976 --> 00:33:47,917
[SPEAKER_01]: After Seattle I was sold to Hollywood and that's where I hurt my arm in 49.

589
00:33:48,217 --> 00:34:01,501
[SPEAKER_01]: I was trying to dick her for a contract and I was late reporting and I think I started just too soon and my whole arm just didn't have it, that's all.

590
00:34:02,121 --> 00:34:10,863
[SPEAKER_01]: So I finished up in 49 and I tried it back in 50 with San Diego but it didn't come around so I just

591
00:34:11,547 --> 00:34:14,730
[SPEAKER_01]: I called it a career and came home and went to work.

592
00:34:14,770 --> 00:34:18,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the bowling alley here, is this your own, when did this begin?

593
00:34:18,232 --> 00:34:26,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I worked in the drugstore until about 1960 and then we concocted the idea of putting up a bowling alley.

594
00:34:26,479 --> 00:34:36,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So we had a little eight lane house down off on Neville Street and it's about the time the automatic pin setters were really in vogue.

595
00:34:37,067 --> 00:34:37,487
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

596
00:34:39,028 --> 00:34:45,132
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought we could use a new establishment, so I got some people interested in what this building got.

597
00:34:46,973 --> 00:34:49,835
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been working here ever since until July the 15th.

598
00:34:51,035 --> 00:34:57,559
[SPEAKER_01]: And on July the 15th, we sold to this boy here and another boy about his age.

599
00:34:58,920 --> 00:35:02,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And all I'm doing now is just finishing up my part of it, and I'm through.

600
00:35:05,124 --> 00:35:07,005
[SPEAKER_01]: I am finished as of nothing.

601
00:35:09,316 --> 00:35:09,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

602
00:35:10,017 --> 00:35:12,619
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just going to loaf and fish.

603
00:35:13,359 --> 00:35:14,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, isn't that wonderful?

604
00:35:14,780 --> 00:35:18,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm 59 years old and I thought, well, I've got to give it up.

605
00:35:18,843 --> 00:35:19,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Hello, Harry.

606
00:35:19,363 --> 00:35:23,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Excuse me.

607
00:35:23,986 --> 00:35:24,847
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees people.

608
00:35:24,887 --> 00:35:28,849
[SPEAKER_01]: His wife bowled with me for years and he's quite a fisherman.

609
00:35:28,890 --> 00:35:29,870
[SPEAKER_01]: He's retired.

610
00:35:30,671 --> 00:35:34,954
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes down to North Carolina and down to Virginia and he's always kidding me about all the fish he catches.

611
00:35:34,974 --> 00:35:36,855
[SPEAKER_01]: I call him Catfish Harry.

612
00:35:38,015 --> 00:35:39,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But I won't be here.

613
00:35:40,614 --> 00:35:42,114
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned fishing.

614
00:35:42,854 --> 00:35:45,035
[SPEAKER_00]: In your off seasons, is that what you did a lot of?

615
00:35:45,075 --> 00:35:50,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I used to do a lot of fishing, and I used to do a lot of bowling while I was playing ball, and I did a lot of hunting.

616
00:35:50,916 --> 00:35:55,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I opened this bowling alley, it tied me down so that I couldn't get out.

617
00:35:55,577 --> 00:36:02,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And I opened it in 1960, and for 14 years, I've done nothing but work, from 9 o'clock in the morning to late at night.

618
00:36:03,099 --> 00:36:05,960
[SPEAKER_01]: I had no time for anything else.

619
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:07,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's reached the point now where

620
00:36:08,370 --> 00:36:23,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I just don't have the steam I used to, that's all, and my legs won't carry me that many hours again, and I couldn't see my way clear when I could find some decent help, so I... Yeah, it's probably the best thing to do, though.

621
00:36:23,802 --> 00:36:27,124
[SPEAKER_02]: Well... Now you can enjoy life, and you're young enough to enjoy it.

622
00:36:27,144 --> 00:36:27,565
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

623
00:36:27,745 --> 00:36:29,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what my wife and I have decided.

624
00:36:31,127 --> 00:36:33,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you've got to call it quits sometime.

625
00:36:34,430 --> 00:36:36,992
[SPEAKER_01]: The biggest decision I had to make was baseball.

626
00:36:37,922 --> 00:36:38,603
[SPEAKER_01]: giving it up.

627
00:36:39,623 --> 00:36:46,789
[SPEAKER_01]: And heck, when you give up your first love and the only thing you've ever known how to do, then this is not hard for me.

628
00:36:46,929 --> 00:36:50,592
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I can... What did your father do?

629
00:36:51,513 --> 00:36:55,957
[SPEAKER_01]: My father was a boilermaker for the Standard Oil Company in New Jersey, which is now Exxon.

630
00:36:56,677 --> 00:36:58,318
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was... Did he play any ball?

631
00:36:58,539 --> 00:36:58,859
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

632
00:36:59,159 --> 00:37:00,981
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he was an immigrant from Poland.

633
00:37:01,661 --> 00:37:02,382
[SPEAKER_01]: He came to New Jersey...

634
00:37:02,402 --> 00:37:05,024
[SPEAKER_00]: I noticed your name was spelt G-O-R-S-Y.

635
00:37:06,700 --> 00:37:12,223
[SPEAKER_01]: My original name, the spelled Polish name, is G-O-R-C-Z-Y-C-A.

636
00:37:13,384 --> 00:37:29,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But for the convenience of the sportswriters, I tried to change it to G-O-R-S-C-Y-C-A, because I have an older brother who played ball in semi-pro, and that's what he used, and I just tried to use the same name.

637
00:37:29,292 --> 00:37:34,435
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I came down here, instead of using the C, they injected an E.

638
00:37:35,997 --> 00:37:39,399
[SPEAKER_01]: S-E-Y, instead of saying Gorsica, they would say Gorsica.

639
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:43,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that didn't set too well with me, but after a while I got accustomed to it.

640
00:37:44,963 --> 00:37:50,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for the sportswriters' convenience, I just used G-O-R-S-I-C-A, just like you say it.

641
00:37:51,528 --> 00:37:52,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Gorsica.

642
00:37:52,728 --> 00:37:54,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you have to go through some legal change?

643
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:55,390
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

644
00:37:56,411 --> 00:38:00,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Your name is... No, I still keep my original name and social security number.

645
00:38:00,053 --> 00:38:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all that matters.

646
00:38:01,354 --> 00:38:01,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

647
00:38:02,155 --> 00:38:05,137
[SPEAKER_00]: But in business, like, I've used Gorsica.

648
00:38:05,897 --> 00:38:07,859
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't.

649
00:38:07,879 --> 00:38:09,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Was your dad pretty proud of you?

650
00:38:09,860 --> 00:38:10,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was.

651
00:38:11,041 --> 00:38:17,686
[SPEAKER_01]: But like all fathers, a boy gets big enough in a family of 10 kids, why, he thought he ought to go to work.

652
00:38:17,806 --> 00:38:19,307
[SPEAKER_01]: And playing ball wasn't work.

653
00:38:20,708 --> 00:38:21,709
[SPEAKER_01]: That was too much fun.

654
00:38:22,990 --> 00:38:24,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he see you pitch much?

655
00:38:24,131 --> 00:38:24,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

656
00:38:24,891 --> 00:38:26,913
[SPEAKER_01]: He saw me pitch in the World League Leagues.

657
00:38:26,933 --> 00:38:29,295
[SPEAKER_01]: He used to come to New York when we played the Yankees.

658
00:38:29,355 --> 00:38:33,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was fortunate enough to play in several games in the stadium.

659
00:38:33,358 --> 00:38:34,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, great.

660
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:38,701
[SPEAKER_01]: How about your mother?

661
00:38:38,721 --> 00:38:40,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Was she living there?

662
00:38:40,462 --> 00:38:44,884
[SPEAKER_01]: No, she was living then, yes, but she never did come out to the park.

663
00:38:46,425 --> 00:38:48,066
[SPEAKER_01]: My dad was a baseball fan.

664
00:38:49,427 --> 00:38:52,068
[SPEAKER_01]: He never missed a game, any chance he had.

665
00:38:56,533 --> 00:38:59,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Wonderful of you to see me coming in so unannounced.

666
00:38:59,338 --> 00:39:00,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm glad I was here.

667
00:39:00,821 --> 00:39:02,323
[SPEAKER_00]: By golly, you're lucky you caught me.

668
00:39:02,343 --> 00:39:06,511
[SPEAKER_00]: If you happen to have any spare pictures around you could autograph them.

669
00:39:06,551 --> 00:39:08,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see, I might have some somewhere.

670
00:39:08,554 --> 00:39:09,236
[SPEAKER_01]: That'd be nice.

